{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787458142,"nonce":"QRqR7ESMmEb8v6+AeKcmS2TSXhs=","title":"`durability_tier` claims the Object tier for ANY S3 configuration without","message":"probing it, so a nonexistent or unreachable bucket still licenses `Succeeded`.\n\nWhere — `crates/scarab-server/src/workspaced.rs:391-396`:\n\n    fn durability_tier(warm_dir, store) -\u003e io::Result\u003cDurabilityTier\u003e {\n        match store {\n            StoreConfig::S3(_) =\u003e Ok(DurabilityTier::Object),\n            StoreConfig::LocalDir(dir) =\u003e { ...st_dev probe... }\n\nThe `LocalDir` arm does real work: it creates the warm dir and compares `st_dev`\nto decide whether warm and cold are genuinely different devices. The `S3` arm\ndoes none — the mere presence of config is taken as proof of an object tier.\n\nWhy it matters: this value is the whole ADR-0064 disclosure chain. It is\nreported by the flush, mapped onto a Pod annotation in the same patch as the\nsnapshot root, read by the scheduler on `Succeeded`, and persisted to\n`attempts.output_durability` in the same transaction as the root. Every link is\nbuilt and correct — and the first link can be a lie.\n\nFailure scenario: an operator sets `SCARAB_S3_BUCKET` to a bucket that does not\nexist, or whose credentials are wrong, or in a region the pod cannot reach.\nBoot succeeds. Runs go green. `attempts.output_durability` reads `object` for\nevery attempt. There is no `warm-only` banner, because the warm-only path was\nnever taken. Evidence that only ever existed on a warm volume is recorded as\ndurable, and the discrepancy surfaces only when someone tries to read a\nsnapshot after the volume is gone — at which point the stamp says it should\nhave been there.\n\nNote the deliberate contrast: boot DOES gate on other things\n(`MissingS3Credentials` is refused even under `SCARAB_DEV_INSECURE`), so the\nproject's posture is to fail closed on storage misconfiguration. This is the\none place it does not.\n\nFix: probe the bucket at startup (a HEAD on the bucket, or a write-read-delete\nof a sentinel key) and fall back to `WarmOnly` with the existing disclosure\nbanner when it fails. Prefer degrading loudly over asserting silently — that is\nwhat the `LocalDir` arm already does.","files":null}]}